{:edb/db-before-t 1108 :edb/db-after-t 1109 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534421 50 #inst "2026-10-11T20:33:12.478Z" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122643 13194139534421 17592186045417 false] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122644 13194139534421 17592186045417 false] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122645 13194139534421 17592186045417 false] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122646 13194139534421 17592186045417 false] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122647 13194139534421 17592186045417 false] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420920 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420922 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420925 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420926 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186122642 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420927 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186122642 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420927 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186124130 1078 #edb/ref 17592186124131 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186124451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420930 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186125007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186125007 1071 "Let $f(x, y, z) = e^{xy}\\cos (z)$. Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0, 1, π)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534421 17592186045417 false] [17592186125007 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi ).$" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186125007 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,1,\\pi )=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi )=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi )=0, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-{e}^{xy}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,1,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,1,\\pi ) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{y}(0,1,\\pi ),{f}_{z}(0,1,\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,\\pi )+∇f(0,1,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,0,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z-\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186125255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186125255 1071 "Suppose that $f(x, y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n$f(2,-2,-1) = 4, f_{x}(2,-2,-1) = 2, f_{y}(2,-2,-1) =-2, f_{z}(2,-2,-1) =-4$.\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534421 17592186045417 false] [17592186125255 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,-2,-1)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1)=2,\\,{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186125255 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,-2,-1)+∇f(2,-2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,-2,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.02,$ $y=-1.98,$ and $z=-1.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05) & ≈4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2.02-2,-1.98+2,-1.05+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =4+(0.04-0.04+0.2) \\\\  & =4.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420931 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420932 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420933 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420934 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420935 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1045 "Linearization of Multivariable Functions" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1046 #edb/ref 17592186420933 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049809 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420918 1053 #uuid "e616bf48-afee-5693-88d5-4ed73b51c3cc" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420919 1080 "f(x,y)≈-2x-y" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420919 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420919 1083 #uuid "19a5d7fe-a63b-5e32-949d-b3f2969e0db8" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420920 1080 "f(x,y,z)≈\\pi -1-x-z" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420920 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420920 1083 #uuid "3996a57d-3ca8-524e-b713-371dae9f92c2" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420921 1080 "f(x,y)≈-1-2x+y" 13194139534421 17592186045417 true] [17592186420921 1